sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4 + 2 (DNS hold), firewall.allow shape, Gateway signs CSRs (root-of-trust), single-swamp-node. docs/cli/route.mdx, docs/reference/routes.md (Change an existing Route).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: route:update
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Route Nodes<br/>workload · Router
  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS
  CLI->>GW: route:update 14<br/>--domain=shop.example.test
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit route:update 14 --domain=shop.example.test`
  %%> - syntax: `<route> [--domain=DOMAIN] [--publication=private|public] [--web-root=PATH]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - Route 14, with at least one option
  %%> - `--web-root` goes on its own; `--web-root=` clears it
  %%> Three changes:
  %%> - domain: a replacement Route takes over, so you get a new Route ID
  %%> - publication: the public edge opens or closes; the Route ID stays
  %%> - web root: the Caddy site serves another directory; the Route ID stays
  Note over GW: Validate, lock, record<br/>Route 15 pending · task #5160
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 7, the Node of the Route's target
  %%> - Route 14 is an explicit app Route; generated domains change only through `instance:rename`
  %%> - the new domain is valid, free, and not reserved
  %%> - no other domain change runs for Route 14 (`route.domain_change_conflict`)
  %%> - a public Route needs production targets and a serving Ingress
  %%> Locks:
  %%> - the projection lock and the environment owner of every target Instance
  %%> - a busy lock is waited for up to 30 s, then `app-dev.projection_busy`
  %%> Result:
  %%> - for a domain change: replacement Route 15 recorded as `pending`, with the same Project, scope, targets and publication
  %%> - Route 14 stays `active` and is still the only Route that serves
  %%> - task #5160 recorded with the full intent; nothing has happened on a Node yet
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-route-update
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the rendered Caddy blocks before and after, per Node
  %%> - the certificate leaves, LAN tuples, DNS records and probes
  %%> - the new `APP_URL`; production `.env` material only as vault key names (R3)
  %%> - each Node's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5160
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  opt domain changes
    SW->>N: Make a CSR on the Node<br/>openssl req · key stays on Node
    %%> How:
    %%> - Swamp reaches Node 7, and the Router for a Cluster Route, over `@swamp/ssh` from 209
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - makes a new key and a certificate request for `shop.example.test`, under Route 15's own scope
    %%> Why its own scope:
    %%> - the old site keeps its certificate until cleanup, so both names serve during the change
    SW->>GW: Sign the CSR<br/>operation:direct:certificate-sign
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - sends the CSRs with task #5160 and the run id
    %%> Why:
    %%> - the Orbit CA key stays in the Gateway; signing is the one step that calls the Gateway
    GW-->>SW: Signed certificate<br/>Orbit CA key stays in Gateway
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - one public certificate bundle per leaf
    SW->>N: Install the certificate<br/>/etc/caddy/orbit-certificates
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - writes the certificate next to its key, owned `root:caddy`, mode 0750, and checks it
  end
  SW->>N: Publish the Caddy site<br/>caddy validate, then reload
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - domain: adds the new site next to the old one
  %%> - web root: points the site at the new directory, after the web-root access step for a development target
  %%> - validates the whole Caddyfile, then reloads Caddy; never a restart (rule 7)
  %%> Refuses:
  %%> - a validation error; the old Caddyfile stays in place
  opt Laravel target, new URL
    SW->>N: Write the new APP_URL<br/>route-environment-write
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - development: sets `APP_URL` in `.env` and in the cached configuration
    %%> - production: renders the stored environment against the new Route and replaces only the home's `.env`
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - Composer, Artisan, deploy or restart commands
  end
  SW->>N: Probe the site over TLS<br/>openssl s_client · SNI domain
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - connects from 209 to the workload, or the Router, on port 443 with the new domain as SNI
  %%> - requires a chain the Orbit root trusts
  %%> Why:
  %%> - nothing moves to the new state before the site serves
  opt publication changes
    SW->>N: Open or close the edge<br/>Ingress Caddy · ufw 80/443
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - public: publishes the Ingress site, then opens `orbit:ingress-http` and `orbit:ingress-https`
    %%> - private: withdraws the Ingress site; closes both rules when no public Route is left in the Cluster
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - the public DNS record and Let's Encrypt issuance; Caddy retries issuance on its own
  end
  opt domain changes
    SW->>V: Move private DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - publishes `shop.example.test` and removes `shop.test`
    %%> - checks the loaded catalog and a DNS reply
    %%> Cutover:
    %%> - from here the new domain is the one that serves; a later failure recovers forward
    Note over V: open: Nick decides<br/>DNS hold · native-dns-takeover
    %%> Open:
    %%> - DNS publication is under the DNS hold; Nick decides (rule 9)
    %%> - until then, the run skips this step and Route work stays same-Node work
    Note over SW: Wait the DNS grace<br/>31 s: TTL 30 s + 1 s
    %%> Why:
    %%> - resolvers that honor the TTL stop asking for the old name before its site leaves
    %%> How:
    %%> - a native wait in the run; it holds no Gateway lock
    SW->>N: Withdraw the old site<br/>caddy reload · certificate rm
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - removes Route 14's site blocks and reloads Caddy
    %%> - then removes Route 14's certificate scopes and the LAN tuples no other Route needs
  end
  alt every step succeeds
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event + outputs
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record result<br/>Route 15 active · 14 deleted
    %%> Result:
    %%> - domain: Route 15 becomes `active`; Route 14 is deleted and its domain released
    %%> - publication or web root: Route 14 keeps its ID and gets the new value
    %%> - task #5160 succeeds
  else a step fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed, its error code, and whether DNS had moved
    Note over GW: Mark the change failed<br/>old state kept · rerun resumes
    %%> Result before the DNS move:
    %%> - Route 15 is marked `failed`; Route 14 stays `active` and unchanged
    %%> - the new site stays on the Node; it serves no name, so it is harmless
    %%> Result after the DNS move:
    %%> - Route 15 serves; Route 14 is `retiring` until a rerun finishes cleanup
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again; it skips finished steps and continues
    %%> - to abandon a change that never moved DNS, run `route:destroy 15`
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5160
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
